<% content_for :title, "Multi-Tenant Uptime Monitoring | StatusPulse" %>
<% content_for :description, "Isolate endpoints, incidents, users, reports, and public status pages by client workspace while monitoring everything from one console." %>

<aside class="mk-railstrip" aria-hidden="true" data-controller="rail">
  <span class="mk-railstrip__mark"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="22 12 18 12 15 21 9 3 6 12 2 12"></polyline></svg></span>
  <div class="mk-railstrip__bars" data-rail-target="bars"></div>
  <span class="mk-railstrip__label">Tenant scoped</span>
</aside>

<div class="mk-shell">
<header class="mk-nav mk-wrap">
  <a href="<%= root_path %>" class="mk-nav__brand"><span class="mk-nav__mark"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="22 12 18 12 15 21 9 3 6 12 2 12"></polyline></svg></span><span class="mk-nav__word">StatusPulse</span></a>
  <nav class="mk-nav__links">
    <a href="<%= uptime_monitoring_for_agencies_path %>">Agencies</a>
    <a href="<%= client_uptime_reports_path %>">Reports</a>
    <a href="<%= org_public_status_path(org_slug: "statuspulse") %>">Demo status</a>
    <a href="<%= login_path %>" class="mk-nav__signin">Sign in</a>
    <a href="<%= signup_path %>" class="mk-btn mk-btn--sm">Create workspace</a>
  </nav>
</header>

<main>
  <section class="mk-hero mk-wrap">
    <div class="mk-hero__copy">
      <p class="mk-eyebrow">Multi-tenant uptime monitoring</p>
      <h1 class="mk-h1">Multi-tenant uptime monitoring.<br><span class="mk-h1__outline">Client by client.</span></h1>
      <p class="mk-lede">StatusPulse is organized around client workspaces. Each workspace owns its endpoints, incidents, users, reports, maintenance windows, and public <code class="mk-code">/status/&lt;client&gt;</code> page.</p>
      <div class="mk-hero__actions">
        <a href="<%= signup_path %>" class="mk-btn">Create a workspace</a>
        <a href="<%= uptime_monitoring_for_agencies_path %>" class="mk-btn mk-btn--ghost">See agency workflow</a>
      </div>
      <p class="mk-hero__note">Built for agencies and MSPs that manage more than one client environment.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mk-console" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="mk-console__bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
<pre class="mk-console__code">workspace: northwind
  endpoints: 12
  users: admins, members
  incidents: scoped
  reports: 7d / 30d / 90d
  status: /status/northwind

workspace: meridian
  endpoints: separate
  incidents: separate
  reports: separate</pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mk-section mk-wrap">
    <div class="mk-section__head">
      <div class="mk-ticks" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
      <p class="mk-eyebrow">Isolation model</p>
      <h2 class="mk-h2">The tenant boundary is the workspace.</h2>
      <p class="mk-body">A client workspace is not just a filter in the interface. It is the unit that owns operational records and keeps client context clear.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="mk-grid mk-grid--3">
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">Endpoints</h3><p class="mk-body">HTTP checks belong to one workspace, with method, cadence, timeout, headers, body, and assertion settings kept with that client.</p></article>
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">Incidents</h3><p class="mk-body">Incident records and updates stay attached to the workspace and the services they affected.</p></article>
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">Reports</h3><p class="mk-body">Uptime windows, latency statistics, and weekly digests are produced per workspace so reporting matches the client relationship.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mk-section mk-wrap">
    <div class="mk-section__head">
      <p class="mk-eyebrow">Why it matters</p>
      <h2 class="mk-h2">One shared monitor account does not scale cleanly.</h2>
      <p class="mk-body">Client work introduces boundaries: different stakeholders, different services, different incident timelines, and different reports. StatusPulse keeps those boundaries visible while giving operators one place to work.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="mk-grid mk-grid--3">
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">Less account sprawl</h3><p class="mk-body">Create client workspaces without creating an entirely separate monitoring system for each client.</p></article>
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">Cleaner communication</h3><p class="mk-body">Give stakeholders a status URL for their workspace instead of exposing unrelated services.</p></article>
      <article class="mk-card"><h3 class="mk-h3">Cleaner operations</h3><p class="mk-body">Operators can triage incidents and review reports with client context already attached.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mk-cta mk-wrap">
    <h2 class="mk-h2">Monitor many clients without flattening them into one system.</h2>
    <p class="mk-body">Start with one workspace, then add client environments as your portfolio grows.</p>
    <a href="<%= signup_path %>" class="mk-btn mk-btn--lg">Create a workspace</a>
  </section>
</main>

<footer class="mk-footer mk-wrap">
  <span class="mk-footer__word">StatusPulse</span>
  <nav class="mk-footer__links">
    <a href="<%= root_path %>">Home</a>
    <a href="<%= uptime_monitoring_for_agencies_path %>">Agencies</a>
    <a href="<%= client_uptime_reports_path %>">Reports</a>
    <a href="<%= org_public_status_path(org_slug: "statuspulse") %>">Status</a>
    <a href="https://github.com/craigderington/statuspulse" rel="noopener">Source</a>
  </nav>
</footer>
</div>
